<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Brazil</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1654 CE · capital Mauritsstad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch West India Company's colony in northeastern Brazil, which under Johan Maurits briefly made Recife a centre of art, science and religious tolerance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the Eighty Years' War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which then ruled Portugal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> coast. After a failed occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> in 1624 and 1625, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-pernambuco">Pernambuco</a> in 1630 and gradually extended control along the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergipe">Sergipe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen">Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</a> governed from 1637 to 1644. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritsstad">Mauritsstad</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production and seized Portuguese slave-trading posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He brought the painters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-post">Frans Post</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-eckhout">Albert Eckhout</a> and the naturalists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-marcgraf">Georg Marcgraf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-piso">Willem Piso</a>, whose work was the first scientific study of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>. Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> enjoyed toleration, and Recife's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahal-zur-israel-synagogue">Kahal Zur Israel</a> was the first synagogue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen">Johan Maurits</a> left, Portuguese planters rose in 1645 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-guararapes">Battles of Guararapes</a> in 1648 and 1649. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> surrendered in 1654, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-hague-1661">Treaty of The Hague</a> in 1661 the Dutch renounced <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> for a payment. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Recife later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-brazil.jpg" alt="Dutch Brazil" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Giro720 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Holland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1630 CE – 1654 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mauritsstad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church, Roman Catholicism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Holland, Nieuw Holland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=dutch-brazil" aria-label="Dutch Brazil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3716 2479 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Brazil lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4036 2735L4036 2733M4036 2735L5097 634M4036 2735L5097 634M4036 2735L3944 2917"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4036 2733h0M5097 634h0M5097 634h0M3944 2917h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4036 2735h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4036 2735h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=dutch-brazil">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

